  "slot_count": 51,
  "slots": [
    {
      "decision": "Finite-dimensional real Hilbert-type ambient space with Borel measurable structure; V, nonnegative alpha/beta and real eta are input data.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[00] Ambient data.\nLet E be a finite-dimensional real inner product space, equipped with its normed additive commutative group structure and a measurable space equal to its Borel measurable space. Let V : E -> R, let alpha and beta be nonnegative real numbers (Lean type R>=0), and let eta be a real number. Write alpha and beta for their real coercions in real formulas. The theorem has exactly the following six caller conditions.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "ambient-input",
      "slot": "00",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Strict positivity is on the real coercion of alpha.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[01] Caller condition 1: alpha > 0.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "caller-condition",
      "slot": "01",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "The nonnegative parameters satisfy alpha <= beta; this is distinct from either Hessian bound.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[02] Caller condition 2: alpha <= beta.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "caller-condition",
      "slot": "02",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Global C2 Frechet regularity; no extra derivatives or local domain.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[03] Caller condition 3: V is twice continuously differentiable on all of E, namely ContDiff R 2 V.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "caller-condition",
      "slot": "03",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "One universally quantified x,v pair carries the conjunction of lower and upper Hessian quadratic-form bounds.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[04] Caller condition 4: for every x,v in E, the second Frechet derivative satisfies both inequalities\n  alpha * ||v||^2 <= ((D(DV)(x))(v))(v),\n  ((D(DV)(x))(v))(v) <= beta * ||v||^2.\nBoth inequalities have the same globally quantified x and v; they bound the Hessian quadratic form, not an operator norm or a derivative at one distinguished point.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "caller-condition",
      "slot": "04",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Strict positivity of eta is a public input condition.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[05] Caller condition 5: eta > 0.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "caller-condition",
      "slot": "05",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "The exact upper product constraint is beta*eta <= 1.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[06] Caller condition 6: beta * eta <= 1.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "caller-condition",
      "slot": "06",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Only the first six named condition binders are caller premises. Every later existential and all internal instances are concluded or defined.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[07] Boundary between premises and conclusions.\nThe ambient types/structures and the six conditions above are the inputs. All objects under later existential quantifiers, all identities involving them, their positivity/invertibility/isometry properties, and the centered-input constructions below are conclusions. Internal measurable-space and Hilbert-space instances introduce no additional caller conditions. In particular the proposition supplies a kernel, its operator realization, an invertible restricted positive root, and the subsequent polar and functional identities; the caller is not required to supply them.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "scope-decision",
      "slot": "07",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "tilted is normalized exponential tilt; the probability property is subsequently concluded.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[08] Normalized reference measure.\nSet mu = volume.tilted(-V). Thus mu is the normalized exponential tilt of Lebesgue volume with weight exp(-V(x)); the notation is not an unnormalized density. The theorem will assert that this measure is a probability measure.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-measure-definition",
      "slot": "08",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "The input to the pushforward is (X,Z); its output is (X,X+sqrt(eta)Z), and nu is the second marginal.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[09] Joint Gaussian pushforward and its observed marginal.\nSet\n  J = (mu product stdGaussian(E)).map((x,z) |-> (x, x + sqrt(eta) z)),\n  nu = J.snd.\nEquivalently J is the joint law of (X,Y) obtained from independent X with law mu and standard Gaussian Z on E, with Y = X + sqrt(eta) Z. The measure nu is the law of the second coordinate Y. This is the actual joint measure defining all subsequent joint L2 spaces and conditional expectations.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-joint-definition",
      "slot": "09",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Lambda uses (x,y)->(y,2x-y), while F uses (x,y)->(x,2x-y). Keep the distinct first coordinates.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[10] Two distinct reflection maps.\nDefine the reflected-pair law and the transformation of the original joint space by\n  Lambda = J.map((x,y) |-> (y, 2x-y)),\n  F(x,y) = (x, 2x-y).\nThe first map has first coordinate y; F keeps first coordinate x. They must not be interchanged. Scalar 2 means real scalar multiplication in E.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-map-definitions",
      "slot": "10",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "HP is the closed sigma(Y)-measurable subspace of joint L2, with internally derived sigma-algebra inclusion.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[11] Conditional measurable subspace.\nOn E product E use the product Borel measurable space. Let mY be the pullback of the measurable space on E by the second-coordinate projection, namely sigma(Y). Its inclusion in the product measurable space is supplied internally by measurability of that projection. Put\n  H = L2(J; R), K = L2(nu; R),\n  HP = lpMeas(R,R,mY,2,J),\nwhere HP is the closed Hilbert subspace of H consisting of sigma(Y)-measurable equivalence classes. Write iP : HP -> H for its inclusion.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-subspace-definition",
      "slot": "11",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "condExpL2 lands in HP; P includes it into H. M is the actual measure-preserving second-coordinate pullback.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[12] Conditional expectation and pullback.\nLet EY : H -> HP denote the displayed condExpL2 for J and mY, and set P = iP composed with EY, a bounded real-linear endomorphism of H. The second projection is internally supplied as a measure-preserving map from J to nu. Its isometric linear pullback is\n  M : K -> H, M u = the class of ((x,y) |-> u(y)).\nThe measure-preserving record, the measurable-space inclusion, EY, P and M are internal definitions, not hypotheses to be supplied by the caller.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-operator-definitions",
      "slot": "12",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Three probabilities, exact range equality, and pointwise-in-HP operator fixedness are simultaneous conclusions.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[13] Probability and exact conditional-expectation range.\nThe conclusions include that mu, J and nu are probability measures, that HP is exactly the linear range of P, and that P(iP f) = iP f for every f in HP. The displayed equality P(f)=f uses the inclusion of HP into H on its right side.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-conclusion",
      "slot": "13",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "S is a produced Markov kernel with every-state equality of normalized measures and exact V((y+x)/2), 8*eta constants.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[14] A single every-state normalized kernel.\nThere exists one Markov kernel S : E -> Measure(E) such that, for every y in E, as an equality of measures,\n  S(y) = volume.tilted(x |-> -V((y+x)/2) - ||y-x||^2/(8 eta)).\nIts normalized weight is exp(-V((y+x)/2) - ||y-x||^2/(8 eta)). The argument of V is (y+x)/2 and the quadratic denominator is exactly 8 eta. This normalized formula is asserted for every y, not only nu-almost every y.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "14",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "S disintegrates Lambda in its second coordinate given its first; both Lambda marginals are the same nu.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[15] Conditional reflected law and both marginals.\nThe same S is a conditional kernel for Lambda: it gives the conditional distribution of the second coordinate of Lambda given its first coordinate. Equivalently Lambda is the measure obtained by taking its first marginal and then S. Moreover\n  Lambda.fst = nu, Lambda.snd = nu.\nThus the conditional first marginal is nu and the reflected law has the same measure nu as its second marginal. These are conclusions about the actual Lambda defined in [10].",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-conclusion",
      "slot": "15",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "e is onto HP and its inclusion agrees exactly in Lp with M. Use this very e for every subsequent conjugation.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[16] Produced equivalence onto the conditional subspace.\nThere exists a real-linear isometric equivalence e : K -> HP with\n  iP(e u) = M u for every u in K.\nIn particular e has inverse e.symm : HP -> K; it is onto HP. For an endomorphism C of K, write e C e^(-1) for the induced bounded endomorphism of HP. This conjugation is the isometric star-algebra conjugation in the statement.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "16",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "U is a produced joint linear isometry, an involution, and selfadjoint; its action is observable-wise J-AE pullback by F.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[17] Produced joint reflection operator.\nThere exists a real-linear isometry U : H -> H such that for every g in H,\n  (U g)(x,y) = g(F(x,y)) for J-almost every (x,y).\nThe operator U is involutive, U(U g)=g for every g in H, and its underlying bounded operator is selfadjoint. All later occurrences use this same U. The almost-everywhere set in the representative equality may depend on g.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "17",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "One selfadjoint T contracts every K observable, realizes the same S nu-AE, and preserves its nu integral.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[18] Produced observable Markov operator.\nThere exists a bounded operator T : K -> K which is selfadjoint and, for every u in K, satisfies all three properties\n  ||T u|| <= ||u||,\n  (T u)(y) = integral u(x) dS(y)(x) for nu-almost every y,\n  integral (T u)(y) dnu(y) = integral u(y) dnu(y).\nThe first is an L2 contraction bound, the second uses the very same normalized kernel S, and the third is preservation of the scalar integral. The representative formula is per observable, not a single claimed everywhere pointwise identity for all observables simultaneously.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "18",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "A compresses U to HP; B has domain HP and ambient codomain H, retaining the exact (I-P)UP factorization.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[19] Compression and off-diagonal operator.\nDefine bounded operators\n  A : HP -> HP, A = orthogonalProjectionOnto(HP) composed with U composed with iP,\n  B : HP -> H, B = (I_H - P) U P iP.\nHere the orthogonal projection in A has codomain HP. B has ambient joint space H as codomain, even though its values will lie in ker P. The factor P displayed in B is retained.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-operator-definitions",
      "slot": "19",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "A equals eTe^-1 and is a selfadjoint contraction on all HP.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[20] Observable compression conclusions.\nThe theorem asserts\n  A = e T e^(-1),\n  A is selfadjoint,\n  ||A f|| <= ||f|| for every f in HP.\nThe isometric equivalence in [16], not an arbitrary chosen coordinate change, is used here.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-conclusion",
      "slot": "20",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "PBf=0 is proved for every HP input; it does not change the declared codomain of B.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[21] Off-diagonal range conclusion.\nFor every f in HP, P(B f) = 0. Thus B maps into the conditional-expectation kernel as a proved property, without redefining its ambient codomain H.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-conclusion",
      "slot": "21",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Gamma is positive, squares to I-T*T, and commutes with T; it is not supplied by the caller.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[22] Positive root on the observable space.\nThere exists a bounded operator Gamma : K -> K such that\n  Gamma is positive,\n  Gamma Gamma = I_K - T T,\n  Gamma T = T Gamma.\nPositive means selfadjoint with nonnegative quadratic form. The square-root equation concerns I minus the operator square T T, not I minus T.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "22",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "GammaP uses the same conjugation, with transported positivity/root/commutation and exact B*B=GammaP^2.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[23] Transported root and the exact off-diagonal defect.\nSet GammaP = e Gamma e^(-1) : HP -> HP. Then\n  GammaP is positive,\n  GammaP GammaP = I_HP - A A,\n  GammaP A = A GammaP,\n  B.adjoint composed with B = GammaP GammaP.\nThe last identity is an equality of operators HP -> HP. In particular B.adjoint has domain all of H and codomain HP.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-definition-and-conclusions",
      "slot": "23",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "q is the produced nu-AE constant one, is fixed by T, and represents the integral pairing for all K.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[24] Produced constant and integral pairing.\nThere exists q in K with q(y)=1 for nu-almost every y, T q = q, and\n  inner_R(q,u) = integral u(y) dnu(y) for every u in K.\nThese statements refer to one common L2 element q. The pairing has q as its first displayed inner-product argument.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "24",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "qP=e q; HP0 is the kernel of inner(qP,-) inside HP, with inherited complete Hilbert structures.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[25] Centered conditional Hilbert subspace.\nSet qP = e q in HP and\n  HP0 = ker(f |-> inner_R(qP,f)) inside HP.\nThe normed-group, real-inner-product, and complete-space structures on HP0 are the inherited closed-kernel structures, all installed internally. Write i0 : HP0 -> HP for its inclusion. This is a subspace of HP, not a new L2 measure or a separately assumed domain.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-subspace-definition",
      "slot": "25",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Centering is exactly the zero nu integral of e^-1 f, for every f in HP.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[26] Exact meaning of centering.\nFor every f in HP,\n  f belongs to HP0 if and only if integral (e^(-1) f)(y) dnu(y) = 0.\nThe mean is evaluated using the nu observable represented by e^(-1) f. The integral is not an integral of f against an unspecified measure.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-conclusion",
      "slot": "26",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "GammaP kills qP; gamma is exactly 2*sqrt(alpha*eta)/(1+alpha*eta) and is strictly positive.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[27] Constant annihilation and the numeric lower-bound coefficient.\nThe conclusions include GammaP qP = 0. Define the real number\n  gamma = 2 sqrt(alpha eta)/(1 + alpha eta).\nThe theorem asserts gamma > 0. The numerator is exactly 2 sqrt(alpha eta); the denominator is exactly 1 + alpha eta. This gamma is defined from the original caller parameters.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-constant-and-conclusions",
      "slot": "27",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "GammaP0 is the same-root restriction to HP0; positivity, lower bound by gamma I, and being a unit are concluded.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[28] Positive, uniformly positive, invertible centered restriction.\nThere exists a bounded GammaP0 : HP0 -> HP0 such that\n  i0(GammaP0 f) = GammaP(i0 f) for every f in HP0,\n  GammaP0 is positive,\n  GammaP0 - gamma I_HP0 is positive,\n  GammaP0 is a unit in the bounded-endomorphism algebra of HP0.\nThus the lower bound is on the exact centered restriction; in quadratic-form notation the third assertion is inner_R(f,GammaP0 f) >= gamma ||f||^2. These are produced properties rather than extra spectral assumptions.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "28",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Inv is one bounded, selfadjoint two-sided inverse of that GammaP0, with operator norm <=1/gamma.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[29] One common bounded two-sided inverse.\nThere exists a bounded Inv : HP0 -> HP0 satisfying\n  Inv GammaP0 = I_HP0,\n  GammaP0 Inv = I_HP0,\n  ||Inv|| <= 1/gamma,\n  Inv is selfadjoint.\nThe norm is the operator norm. This same Inv is used in the polar factor and in the final functional. Neither a one-sided inverse nor an inverse of a different root suffices.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "29",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "A0 is the same-A restriction; its selfadjointness, root commutation, square-sum identity and inverse commutation are concluded.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[30] Centered restriction of the compression.\nThere exists a bounded A0 : HP0 -> HP0 with\n  i0(A0 u) = A(i0 u) for every u in HP0,\n  A0 is selfadjoint,\n  A0 GammaP0 = GammaP0 A0,\n  A0 A0 + GammaP0 GammaP0 = I_HP0,\n  A0 Inv = Inv A0.\nIn particular A0 is a restriction of the same A, not independently introduced observable dynamics.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "30",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Hperp means ker P, not the global zero-mean space; closed-kernel completeness is internal.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[31] Conditional complement.\nSet Hperp = ker P inside H and give it the inherited normed-group, real-inner-product, and closed-kernel complete-space structures. Write iPerp : Hperp -> H for its inclusion. Membership means P h = 0 in H. Hperp must not be replaced by the larger globally zero-integral subspace of H.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-subspace-definition",
      "slot": "31",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "B0 restricts B to centered HP0 inputs and has exact codomain Hperp.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[32] Intrinsic centered off-diagonal block.\nThere exists a bounded B0 : HP0 -> Hperp such that\n  iPerp(B0 f) = B(i0 f) for every f in HP0.\nIts adjoint B0.adjoint therefore has exact domain Hperp and codomain HP0.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "32",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "V0 uses B0 and the already produced Inv, and factors the same B0 through the same GammaP0.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[33] Produced polar factorization.\nThere exists a bounded V0 : HP0 -> Hperp such that\n  V0 = B0 composed with Inv,\n  B0 = V0 composed with GammaP0.\nThe root and inverse are precisely those already produced in [28]-[29]. This is a factorization into the conditional complement, not merely a formal symbol with a separately assumed construction law.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "33",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "V0*V0=I and norm preservation give an isometry into Hperp; do not infer a claimed onto property or reverse product.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[34] Isometry into, without an onto assertion.\nThe same V0 satisfies\n  V0.adjoint composed with V0 = I_HP0,\n  ||V0 f|| = ||f|| for every f in HP0.\nThese assertions make V0 an isometric embedding into Hperp. There is no assertion that it is onto Hperp and no asserted identity V0 composed with V0.adjoint = I_Hperp.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-conclusion",
      "slot": "34",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "R has all H as domain and ker P as codomain, with inclusion Rg=g-Pg.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[35] Produced ambient residual projection.\nThere exists a bounded R : H -> Hperp such that\n  iPerp(R g) = g - P g for every g in H.\nThe codomain is ker P and the inclusion makes the displayed ambient subtraction precise. R is defined on all joint L2 observables.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-witness-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "35",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "The ambient adjoint B*:H->HP is recovered as i0 B0* R for every joint input.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[36] Full-domain ambient adjoint formula.\nFor every g in H,\n  B.adjoint g = i0(B0.adjoint(R g)).\nBoth sides lie in HP. This relates the ambient adjoint with domain H to the intrinsic adjoint with domain Hperp through the produced R. It is not restricted to inputs already in Hperp or in the range of V0.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-conclusion",
      "slot": "36",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "D=R U iPerp is the residual compression of the same U; its ambient action subtracts P after applying U.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[37] Residual compression of the same involution.\nDefine the bounded D : Hperp -> Hperp by\n  D = R composed with U composed with iPerp.\nFor every h in Hperp the theorem states the ambient representative identity\n  iPerp(D h) = U(iPerp h) - P(U(iPerp h)).\nThe occurrences of U and P are the same global operators already specified.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "internal-definition-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "37",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "The exact negative adjoint intertwining is V0* D= -A0 V0* on every Hperp input, not only the V0 range.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[38] Whole-complement adjoint intertwining.\nAs bounded operators from all of Hperp to HP0,\n  V0.adjoint composed with D = -(A0 composed with V0.adjoint).\nThe domain is the entire conditional complement, not only the range of V0. The minus sign applies to the entire composed operator.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "global-conclusion",
      "slot": "38",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "The final implication quantifies all f in H with zero J integral and produces fP in HP0 as its actual conditional component.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[39] Quantification of centered inputs and their actual conditional means.\nFor every f in H satisfying integral f(z) dJ(z) = 0, there exists fP in HP0 with\n  i0(fP) = EY f in HP.\nOnly the scalar integral of the arbitrary joint input f is assumed in this final implication. Existence and centeredness of its conditional component fP are conclusions; they are not new caller assumptions.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "local-premise-and-produced-witness",
      "slot": "39",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "fperp=Rf and fV=V0* fperp; both adjoint/root inclusion equations refer to these actual objects.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[40] Input residual, corrector and adjoint identities.\nFor that f define\n  fperp = R f in Hperp,\n  fV = V0.adjoint(fperp) in HP0.\nThen both identities hold:\n  B.adjoint(iPerp fperp) = i0(GammaP0 fV),\n  i0(GammaP0 fV) = GammaP(i0 fV).\nThese retain the distinction between the ambient adjoint, the intrinsic root and their inclusions. The corrector fV is actually computed from the joint residual.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "local-definitions-and-conclusions",
      "slot": "40",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "Pythagoras retains ||fperp||^2, while the separate contraction uses ||fV||<=||fperp||.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[41] Input norm decomposition and corrector bound.\nThe theorem asserts\n  ||f||^2 = ||fP||^2 + ||fperp||^2,\n  ||fV|| <= ||fperp||.\nThe first norm is in H, the second in HP0, the third in Hperp; inherited norms make the orthogonal decomposition meaningful.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "local-conclusions",
      "slot": "41",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "g is exactly U(Pf-(f-Pf)); zero J integral of g is concluded from centered f, not assumed.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[42] Actual transformed joint input and its mean.\nDefine in H\n  g = U(P f - (f - P f)).\nThis is also U(2 P f - f), but the first expression is the displayed definition. The theorem concludes integral g(z) dJ(z) = 0. Zero output mean is not an additional input condition and g is not defined by formulas for its future components.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "local-definition-and-conclusion",
      "slot": "42",
      "unresolved": []
    },
    {
      "decision": "gP is the actual conditional component of g; gperp=Rg and gV=V0* gperp are actual residual constructions.",
      "reconstructed_slot_text": "[43] Output conditional mean and residual corrector.\nThere exists gP in HP0 with\n  i0(gP) = EY g in HP.\nDefine\n  gperp = R g in Hperp,\n  gV = V0.adjoint(gperp) in HP0.\nThus gP is the actual conditional-expectation component of the actual g, and gV is the actual adjoint image of its residual. The existence of gP is inside the centered-input conclusion.",
      "resolved": true,
      "role": "local-produced-witness-and-definitions",
      "slot": "43",
      "unresolved": []
    },
